    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d99">

                <pb facs="0196" n="94" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg94"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A5/450: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Chargé in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Atherton</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-08-17T16:00:00-04:00">August 17, 1935—4
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>216. Further reference your 359, August 9, 7 p.m. We are wondering whether, in
                    seeking qualitative limitation even without quantitative limitation, the British
                    have obtained information to the effect that the Japanese have modified their
                    position expressed last year that they could not agree to qualitative without
                    quantitative limitation.<anchor n="52" xml:id="d99-f1"/> We have no word which
                    would indicate that the Japanese have receded from this position. Please
                    endeavor to ascertain whether there has been any change in this respect.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d99-f1" n="52" xml:id="d99-f1-content">
                <p>See memorandum of conversation in the Prime Minister’s office, November 23, 1934,
                        <ref target="frus1934v01#pg_368">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1934, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, p. 368</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
